          %h3#relation_to_deformation_quantization Relation to deformation quantization
          %p
            There does not seem to be in the literature a precise relation between the methods of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            discussed here and methods of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
          %p
            If the task is to quantize a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            as well as
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eli+Hawkins"} Eli Hawkins
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-plectic+geometry"} 2-plectic geometry
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+Lie+algebroid"} Poisson Lie algebroid
            which is induced by the Poisson manifold; Kontsevich’s construction of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            QFT whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            is that Poisson Lie algebroid – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+sigma-model"} Poisson sigma-model
            –, and the symplectic 2-groupoid that Hawkins et al consider is the “extended” geometric quantization over the as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+prequantum+field+theory"} extended prequantum field theory
            associated with this theory.
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+geometric+quantization+of+2d+Chern-Simons+theory"} extended geometric quantization of 2d Chern-Simons theory
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#ordinary_geometric_quantization_of_a_symplectic_manifold Ordinary geometric quantization of a symplectic manifold
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\pi = \omega^{-1})
            an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+groupoid"} symplectic groupoid
            is just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair+groupoid"} pair groupoid
            equipped with the multiplicative form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi s
                      %mo *
                    %mi ω
                    %mo +
                    %msup
                      %mi t
                      %mo *
                    %mover
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s^* \omega + t^* \bar \omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            induces a prequantization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi s
                    %mo *
                  %mi L
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi t
                    %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s^* L + t^* \bar L
            and coresponding polarization of the symplectic groupoid. The resulting
            %span.newWikiWord
              twisted convolution algebra
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/twisted+convolution+algebra"} ?
            is that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+operators"} compact operators
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
